00:02:33.857 --> 00:02:38.977
Valuation on recurring revenue shifts works by comparing a company's fastest growing segment

00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:44.697
to its overall multiple, then testing whether that growth justifies a higher enterprise value

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:48.717
to sales ratio than the peer group still being used on the whole firm.

00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:54.937
You pull the latest quarterly segment breakdown from TMX's investor site, calculate year-over-year

00:02:54.937 --> 00:02:59.837
growth in the data line, and stack the stock's enterprise value to sales multiple against

00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:01.137
pure-play data vendors.

00:03:01.597 --> 00:03:06.297
When the data segment's growth rate exceeds the blended rate by more than 5 percentage points,

00:03:06.797 --> 00:03:09.217
the technique flags potential multiple expansion.

00:03:09.837 --> 00:03:14.097
Think of it like appraising a duplex with the old single-family playbook after the rental

00:03:14.097 --> 00:03:16.317
sweet started paying more than the owner's unit.

00:03:16.857 --> 00:03:21.597
The floor plan did not change, the cash did, and a buyer who ignores the sweet leaves

00:03:21.597 --> 00:03:22.837
the re-rating on the table.

00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:27.997
The approach has worked when organic volume gains accompanied the shift, and it has failed

00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:32.997
when acquisition costs inflated operating expenses faster than the new revenue could offset

00:03:32.997 --> 00:03:37.577
them, which is the gap between adjusted and reported per share figures this quarter.

00:03:38.297 --> 00:03:43.617
Operating expenses rose substantially, driven largely by costs tied to recent acquisitions,

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:47.297
versions, merger-related items, and higher pass-through costs at box.

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:51.697
The U.S. options exchange in which TMX owns a significant interest.

00:03:52.237 --> 00:03:57.217
Excluding those items, the rise in expenses was much more modest, reflecting higher-head

00:03:57.217 --> 00:04:03.057
count, merit raises, incentive-plan costs, and investment in cloud technology, and the

00:04:03.057 --> 00:04:07.537
whole screen runs on the quarterly filing plus a basic spreadsheet with no paid terminal

00:04:07.537 --> 00:04:08.037
required.

00:04:08.037 --> 00:04:13.797
You see adjusted earnings up, reported earnings down, and a data line growing faster than

00:04:13.797 --> 00:04:14.157
the blend.

00:04:14.657 --> 00:04:19.057
Do you pay up for the mixed shift now, or wait until the integration costs roll off?

00:04:19.557 --> 00:04:24.477
I would run that 5% point growth gap first, and only then decide whether the multiple

00:04:24.477 --> 00:04:25.557
has room to expand.

00:04:26.197 --> 00:04:29.677
Remember, isolate the growth line before you trust the blended multiple.

